<p>
&hellip; I&rsquo;ve lost track. I&rsquo;ve been using Jinx for my spelling correction for a while now and really like it. When <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=13943">I first started</a>, my main problem was that the selection procedure described in the documentation didn&rsquo;t work for me. I was almost certain that was because of an interaction with Ivy; my commenters agreed and offered some things to try.
</p>
<p>
After some experimentation, I still wasn&rsquo;t able to get that aspect of Jinx working. I decided to wait for Emacs 31 to revisit the issue. Emacs 31 is here but I still haven&rsquo;t addressed the problem. Most of the reason for that is that I&rsquo;ve developed some efficient muscle memory for dealing with the problem. I have <code>jinx-correct</code> bound to <kbd>Ctrl</kbd>+<kbd>;</kbd> and when I call <code>jinx-correct</code>, I continue holding down <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> and can easily move down in the list of alternatives by pressing <kbd>n</kbd>. Usually the first choice is the one I want so it&rsquo;s only a single press. Then I type <kbd>Return</kbd> to update the text.
</p>
<p>
That seems like a little more work than simply pressing a number but I don&rsquo;t need to move my hands or reach to do it so it seems easy to me. I&rsquo;ll probably do a bit more research and get the default method working when I get a chance but in the mean time, I&rsquo;m pretty happy with how it&rsquo;s working for me now.
</p>
<p>
I&rsquo;m really enjoying how easy it is to put an &ldquo;unknown spelling&rdquo; in a variety of places—the current file, the current directory, or in a personal global dictionary—and am happy I switched. If you haven&rsquo;t tried it yet, you should give it a spin.
</p>
